<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    The generation engine. It acquires analyzer assemblies, has their descriptors read for it, and
    emits a catalogue as C# source.

    It is not a package of its own: it declares no <ReleaseTrain>, so tools/packaging/pack.sh treats
    it as bundled rather than depended upon, and it reaches users inside the `dcat` tool that
    references it (ADR-0017). Its command line lives there too — this project parses nothing.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <!--
      net8.0, not net10.0, because DiagnosticCatalog.Cli is floored there so one build of the tool
      installs on .NET 8 and every newer major (ADR-0017). A net8.0 project cannot reference a
      net10.0 one, so the engine sets the floor as much as the shell does. Nothing here needs a
      newer BCL: the run uses CreateTempSubdirectory and ReplaceLineEndings, both present since
      .NET 7 and .NET 6.
    -->
    <TargetFramework>net8.0</TargetFramework>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
    <GenerateDocumentationFile>false</GenerateDocumentationFile>
    <!--
      CA1305 (specify IFormatProvider) does not apply: every value interpolated into the
      emitted source is already a string, so no culture-sensitive formatting occurs. The
      one date is passed in pre-formatted as ISO 8601 by the caller.
    -->
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);CA1305</NoWarn>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    No Roslyn here, deliberately. Resolving DiagnosticAnalyzer and DiagnosticDescriptor is what
    reading a descriptor is, and reading happens in CatalogGen.Worker — so the reference lives
    there, and neither this assembly nor the `dcat` process that hosts it ever loads Roslyn.

    This does not make the tool package smaller: the worker ships inside it and brings Roslyn along.
    What it moves is the version conflict. Whatever Roslyn an upstream analyzer drags in is resolved
    in a process whose only job is to survive it, and never against the assemblies the tool itself
    is running on.
  -->

  <!--
    The generator's parsing, naming and emitting logic is internal: it is an engine's implementation,
    not an API. Only Job, CatalogRun and RunOutcome are public, which is the whole surface the tool
    needs. The worker sees the internals because it answers the wire contract declared here and
    shares this assembly's title normalisation — the same code has to run on both sides of that
    wire, or a title would be written one way and read back another.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="CatalogGen.UnitTests" />
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="CatalogGen.Worker" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
